<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1003–</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Savoy is a dynasty founded in the early eleventh century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy">Humbert I, Count of Savoy</a>, in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. It became dukes of Savoy in 1416, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, took the royal title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1713 and exchanged it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1720. Its kings led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> and reigned over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1861 until the monarchy was abolished in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy.jpg" alt="House of Savoy" width="320" height="538" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ddurbmonnejg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lesser_coat_of_arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Italy_(1890)_(2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The house was established in 1003 in the historical region of Savoy, which was originally part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and now lies mostly in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy">Humbert I, Count of Savoy</a>, called Biancamano or White Hand, who ruled from 1003 to 1047 or 1048. His ancestry is uncertain, since contemporary documents do not mention his father. The family was traditionally believed to have come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, but more recent research points to the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, where Humbert and his relatives held extensive possessions. Through marriages and diplomacy the family expanded its territory and influence, first as counts of a small Alpine county north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Amadeus VIII was raised by the Holy Roman Emperor Sigismund to Duke of Savoy in 1416. In 1536 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> invaded Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, and Duke Charles III fled to Vercelli. In April 1655, on perhaps false reports of resistance by the Protestant Waldensians to a plan to resettle them, Charles Emmanuel II ordered their general massacre, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmontese-easter">Piedmontese Easter</a>. It aroused indignation across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and prompted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-milton">John Milton</a>'s sonnet On the Late Massacre in Piedmont.</p><p>The house gained the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, but in 1720 was forced to exchange it for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-quadruple-alliance">War of the Quadruple Alliance</a>. Charles Emmanuel III gained lands at the expense of the Austrian-controlled Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In 1796 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> beat Piedmont-Sardinia, and in 1798 French troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and forced Charles Emmanuel IV to abdicate. The kingdom was restored in 1814 and enlarged with the former Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By 1861 the House of Savoy, through its cadet branch Carignano, led the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> until 1946. Its Italian kings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umberto-i">Umberto I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umberto-ii">Umberto II</a>. Victor Emmanuel III appointed Mussolini prime minister on 28 October 1922, and his failure to move against the regime later led to much criticism. Umberto II reigned for only a few weeks before being deposed after the 1946 Italian institutional referendum, when the Italian Republic was proclaimed. The residences of the royal house in and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its driving force was the Kingdom of Sardinia, ruled from Turin by the <b>House of Savoy</b> and guided by its prime minister, Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>House of Savoy</b> the island remained distinct from the much richer mainland of Piedmont, with its silk industry, wine and the growing city of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861, however, its republican content did not suit the monarchy, which kept the <b>House of Savoy</b>'s &quot;Marcia Reale&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>House of Savoy</b>, now kings of the Kingdom of Sardinia, long sought to absorb the enclave.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-masserano">Principality of Masserano</a></li></ul></section>
